  "worked_solution": "To solve the inequality \n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}-5x<0,\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-{x}^{2}-5x\n$$\n\nlies *below* the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-5x & =0 \\\\ -x(x+5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=0$ and $x=-5,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004892/assets/196587385ffa960d79d64d66f6ba14481905dd3de6159ecaec6a8e7bd2ab58b8.png)\n\nThe values marked in blue show all $x$-values where the parabola lies *below* the $x$-axis.\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x<-5$ or $x>0.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
